WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.300
<v Instructor>So first let's do a deep dive into EC2.</v>

2
00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:05.310
EC2 is a very, very big topic,

3
00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:07.320
but hopefully this is something that you already know

4
00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:10.470
at a very high level based on your previous experience.

5
00:00:10.470 --> 00:00:11.880
And I will try to summarize everything

6
00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:14.220
that's going to be important going into the exam.

7
00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:15.770
So first, when you have an EC2, you need to choose

8
00:00:15.770 --> 00:00:18.780
an instance type and they have different families.

9
00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:21.960
You have R, which is for example, R5X.

10
00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:24.030
That is usually for applications that need a lot of RAM.

11
00:00:24.030 --> 00:00:26.490
So for example, in-memory caches would be great.

12
00:00:26.490 --> 00:00:28.650
C, for applications that need a good amount

13
00:00:28.650 --> 00:00:30.090
of CPU for computations.

14
00:00:30.090 --> 00:00:32.100
So they are great for databases as well.

15
00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:33.810
M, which is sharp balance.

16
00:00:33.810 --> 00:00:35.880
So M is for something like medium,

17
00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:38.250
which is for general web applications.

18
00:00:38.250 --> 00:00:41.010
I, which is when you need very good local I/O.

19
00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:42.750
So this is when you have an instance store

20
00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:44.130
attached to your instance.

21
00:00:44.130 --> 00:00:46.770
And this is great for high performance databases.

22
00:00:46.770 --> 00:00:49.350
G, when you need to have a GPU.

23
00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:50.910
So this is when you're doing machine learning

24
00:00:50.910 --> 00:00:52.290
or video rendering.

25
00:00:52.290 --> 00:00:54.600
And then you have different kind of smaller instances

26
00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:57.870
or T2 and T3, which are burstable up to certain capacities.

27
00:00:57.870 --> 00:01:00.300
So they are instances that need to react very quickly

28
00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:02.250
and get good performance in case on spikes

29
00:01:02.250 --> 00:01:04.560
but gets a baseline performance that's average

30
00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:05.700
in the meantime.

31
00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:08.610
And then if you have T2 or T3 unlimited,

32
00:01:08.610 --> 00:01:09.990
you can get unlimited burst

33
00:01:09.990 --> 00:01:12.480
if you have a very specific type of workload.

34
00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:15.060
Finally, if you need to find the right instances for you,

35
00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:17.880
you have this website called ec2instances.info,

36
00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:19.140
which is great to understand

37
00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:21.270
which instance type is right for you.

38
00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:24.630
Okay, so now we know that based on the EC2 we have,

39
00:01:24.630 --> 00:01:25.710
we have different kind of families

40
00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:27.120
and each family is appropriate

41
00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:29.520
for different kind of workloads in terms of

42
00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:32.550
if the institution type is optimized for that workload,

43
00:01:32.550 --> 00:01:34.440
then the workload will finish faster

44
00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:37.200
and we are probably going to pay a lot less.

45
00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:39.870
Next, when you launch these EC2 instances

46
00:01:39.870 --> 00:01:42.450
you have to place them and by default

47
00:01:42.450 --> 00:01:44.460
when you launch an EC2 instance

48
00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:47.820
it's placed randomly in AWS's data center

49
00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:49.890
and you just have it right away.

50
00:01:49.890 --> 00:01:52.800
But you can tell or hint to AWS

51
00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:54.600
where you want your instance to be placed.

52
00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:56.910
And that is the purpose of placement group.

53
00:01:56.910 --> 00:01:58.590
So you have multiple strategies.

54
00:01:58.590 --> 00:02:01.320
The first one is cluster where you want all your instances

55
00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:04.830
to be within the same AZ into a cluster

56
00:02:04.830 --> 00:02:07.050
that will give it a low-latency group.

57
00:02:07.050 --> 00:02:08.760
This is great when you want to perform

58
00:02:08.760 --> 00:02:11.490
some high performance computing, so HPC.

59
00:02:11.490 --> 00:02:13.680
Next you have spread where you want your instances

60
00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:15.840
to be spread across the underlying hardware.

61
00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:17.370
So you don't want it to be on the same rack.

62
00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:21.030
And so it's maximum of seven instances per group per AZ.

63
00:02:21.030 --> 00:02:23.100
And these are for the applications

64
00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:26.160
that needs to not have a hardware failure

65
00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:28.920
that is spread to different instances.

66
00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:31.590
So by having a spread instance group,

67
00:02:31.590 --> 00:02:32.820
instead of placement group

68
00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:34.830
you will have your instances very far away from each other,

69
00:02:34.830 --> 00:02:35.940
they're spread out

70
00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:38.190
and therefore they won't fail all at once.

71
00:02:38.190 --> 00:02:39.990
And then partition is when you have

72
00:02:39.990 --> 00:02:41.940
a higher number of EC2 instances

73
00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:44.520
that you need to place them across different partitions.

74
00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:46.050
And this is when you have, for example,

75
00:02:46.050 --> 00:02:48.750
something like Hadoop, Cassandra, or Kafka.

76
00:02:48.750 --> 00:02:51.870
These are great for a partition placement group strategy.

77
00:02:51.870 --> 00:02:54.420
Okay, it is possible and you need to know this,

78
00:02:54.420 --> 00:02:58.530
to move an instance into or out of a placement group.

79
00:02:58.530 --> 00:03:01.080
First, you need to stop that instance,

80
00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:02.580
then, you need to use the CLI

81
00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:04.860
to modify the instance placement.

82
00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:06.900
So you're saying in this placement group

83
00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:08.580
or out of this placement group.

84
00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:10.980
And then you can start your instance

85
00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:13.320
and it will be in the placement group you need.

86
00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:15.540
It's very important that you know this.

87
00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:18.360
Next, we have the placement group of type cluster

88
00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:19.590
that we need to do a deep dive on.

89
00:03:19.590 --> 00:03:21.960
So this is what a cluster looks like.

90
00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:24.270
All the instances are going to be on the same rack

91
00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:26.220
in the same availability zone.

92
00:03:26.220 --> 00:03:29.070
And the idea is that we get a great network.

93
00:03:29.070 --> 00:03:31.260
So we get 10 gigabytes per second bandwidth

94
00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:32.310
between these instances.

95
00:03:32.310 --> 00:03:36.030
So they can communicate very, very fast with a low latency.

96
00:03:36.030 --> 00:03:37.590
The concept that if the rack fails,

97
00:03:37.590 --> 00:03:40.710
so if the underlying hardware fails, then all the instances,

98
00:03:40.710 --> 00:03:42.750
because they're placed next to each other

99
00:03:42.750 --> 00:03:44.940
they will all fail at the same time.

100
00:03:44.940 --> 00:03:47.250
And when we have such a cluster placement group,

101
00:03:47.250 --> 00:03:48.960
it's great to choose an instance type

102
00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:51.750
that has enhanced networking because they will have

103
00:03:51.750 --> 00:03:54.210
faster networking capability between each other.

104
00:03:54.210 --> 00:03:56.460
So the use case for a cluster placement group

105
00:03:56.460 --> 00:03:59.130
would be a big data job that needs to complete very fast,

106
00:03:59.130 --> 00:04:00.480
high performance computing

107
00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:02.640
or applications that need extremely low-latency

108
00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:04.170
and high network throughput,

109
00:04:04.170 --> 00:04:07.023
but at the risk of failing all at once.

110
00:04:08.010 --> 00:04:10.860
So spread, here it looks like, we have different hardware

111
00:04:10.860 --> 00:04:13.590
across different AZ and each EC2 will be placed

112
00:04:13.590 --> 00:04:17.130
on a different hardware up to seven EC2 per AZ.

113
00:04:17.130 --> 00:04:18.930
And that gives us some good pros.

114
00:04:18.930 --> 00:04:21.360
The first is that you can span across multiple AZ

115
00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:23.520
and the reduced risk of simultaneous failure

116
00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:25.230
is really, really low.

117
00:04:25.230 --> 00:04:28.470
EC2 instances are on a different physical hardware.

118
00:04:28.470 --> 00:04:31.680
Now for the cons, it's up to only seven instances per AZ

119
00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:33.930
per placement groups so you're limited in scale.

120
00:04:33.930 --> 00:04:35.520
And the use case would be for an application

121
00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:37.740
that needs to maximize high availability

122
00:04:37.740 --> 00:04:40.590
or critical applications where each instance failure

123
00:04:40.590 --> 00:04:43.080
must be isolated from each other.

124
00:04:43.080 --> 00:04:44.970
So this is the safest kind of deployment

125
00:04:44.970 --> 00:04:46.590
for your application.

126
00:04:46.590 --> 00:04:48.960
And partition is a little bit different.

127
00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:52.200
So your AZ will be divided in multiple partitions

128
00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:54.300
and then your EC2 instances will be spread

129
00:04:54.300 --> 00:04:56.070
across all these partitions

130
00:04:56.070 --> 00:04:58.590
and you get up to seven partitions per AZ

131
00:04:58.590 --> 00:05:00.900
and that gives you hundreds of EC2 instances

132
00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:03.360
for the partition kind of placement group.

133
00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:06.090
And the instances in the partition do not share racks

134
00:05:06.090 --> 00:05:07.890
with the instances in other partitions.

135
00:05:07.890 --> 00:05:11.310
So you have, again, some separation of hardware.

136
00:05:11.310 --> 00:05:13.860
A partition failure can affect many EC2

137
00:05:13.860 --> 00:05:16.830
but it won't affect other partitions.

138
00:05:16.830 --> 00:05:19.170
And EC2 instances can get access

139
00:05:19.170 --> 00:05:20.790
to which partition they belong to

140
00:05:20.790 --> 00:05:23.460
thanks to the EC2 metadata service.

141
00:05:23.460 --> 00:05:25.920
So the use cases for this would be HDFS, HBase,

142
00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:27.840
Cassandra, Kafka.

143
00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:31.830
Next, we have several ways of launching an EC2 instance.

144
00:05:31.830 --> 00:05:33.540
So we have on demand instances

145
00:05:33.540 --> 00:05:35.730
and this is when you have a short workload

146
00:05:35.730 --> 00:05:37.710
and you're going to get predictable pricing

147
00:05:37.710 --> 00:05:40.170
and you wanna have reliability.

148
00:05:40.170 --> 00:05:43.620
Instead, if you wanna go for very cheap instances

149
00:05:43.620 --> 00:05:47.250
for short workloads as well, you can use spot instances.

150
00:05:47.250 --> 00:05:49.560
In that case, you're going to get a deep discount

151
00:05:49.560 --> 00:05:52.260
but you run the chance of losing instances

152
00:05:52.260 --> 00:05:55.560
meaning you cannot run reliable long workloads

153
00:05:55.560 --> 00:05:56.850
on spot instances.

154
00:05:56.850 --> 00:05:59.550
But if it's resilient to failure, it's a great choice.

155
00:06:00.540 --> 00:06:01.950
Next, we have reserved instances.

156
00:06:01.950 --> 00:06:03.750
The minimum period is one year.

157
00:06:03.750 --> 00:06:06.360
So the reserved instances are for long workloads

158
00:06:06.360 --> 00:06:08.940
and you want to get big discount

159
00:06:08.940 --> 00:06:10.110
because you know, for example,

160
00:06:10.110 --> 00:06:13.140
you're going to run an EC2 instances for three years.

161
00:06:13.140 --> 00:06:15.360
You also have convertible reserved instances.

162
00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:17.490
So this time, again, you workload is long

163
00:06:17.490 --> 00:06:20.250
but you wanna convert the instance type over time.

164
00:06:20.250 --> 00:06:22.050
Therefore, it's a bit more flexible

165
00:06:22.050 --> 00:06:23.970
with a bit less discounts.

166
00:06:23.970 --> 00:06:26.490
You have different level of payments.

167
00:06:26.490 --> 00:06:28.680
So if you pay everything upfront,

168
00:06:28.680 --> 00:06:30.900
you're going to get a deeper discount.

169
00:06:30.900 --> 00:06:32.730
If you pay partial upfront,

170
00:06:32.730 --> 00:06:35.040
you're going to get less discounts,

171
00:06:35.040 --> 00:06:37.770
and no upfront even less discounts.

172
00:06:37.770 --> 00:06:40.530
So your payment plan really impacts as well

173
00:06:40.530 --> 00:06:42.120
the amount of discount you're going to get

174
00:06:42.120 --> 00:06:43.413
for these instances.

175
00:06:44.460 --> 00:06:47.520
Dedicated instances will provide you the guarantee

176
00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:50.490
that no other customers will share your hardware,

177
00:06:50.490 --> 00:06:54.510
and dedicated host, books you an entire physical server

178
00:06:54.510 --> 00:06:57.480
on which you can control the instance placements.

179
00:06:57.480 --> 00:07:00.030
The use case for them is software licenses

180
00:07:00.030 --> 00:07:03.120
that operate at a core or CPU socket level.

181
00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:05.160
And you can define host affinity

182
00:07:05.160 --> 00:07:09.273
so that the instances reboots are kept on the same host.

183
00:07:10.350 --> 00:07:12.320
You also need to know what is Graviton.

184
00:07:12.320 --> 00:07:14.580
So Graviton processors deliver you

185
00:07:14.580 --> 00:07:17.490
the best price performance for your EC2 instances

186
00:07:17.490 --> 00:07:20.880
and that is for newer generation kind of instances.

187
00:07:20.880 --> 00:07:24.120
So this supports Linux OS, Amazon Linux 2,

188
00:07:24.120 --> 00:07:27.120
RedHat, SUSE, Ubuntu, and so on.

189
00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:29.940
And it is not available for Windows instances,

190
00:07:29.940 --> 00:07:31.200
remember this.

191
00:07:31.200 --> 00:07:33.030
You have Graviton2 which gives you

192
00:07:33.030 --> 00:07:35.160
40% better price performance

193
00:07:35.160 --> 00:07:39.480
over comparable fifth generation x86-based instances.

194
00:07:39.480 --> 00:07:41.460
And Graviton3 which gives you

195
00:07:41.460 --> 00:07:44.430
three times better performance compared to Graviton2.

196
00:07:44.430 --> 00:07:46.770
So the use case is, is that you wanna have good performance

197
00:07:46.770 --> 00:07:47.730
at a better price.

198
00:07:47.730 --> 00:07:50.070
So this could be app servers, microservices,

199
00:07:50.070 --> 00:07:52.350
high performance computing, machine learning,

200
00:07:52.350 --> 00:07:56.610
video encoding, gaming, in-memory caches, and so on.

201
00:07:56.610 --> 00:07:59.610
Okay, now for including metrics in EC2,

202
00:07:59.610 --> 00:08:01.830
we know there is CPU where you get digitalization

203
00:08:01.830 --> 00:08:05.040
as well as the credit usage and balance for T2 Micros.

204
00:08:05.040 --> 00:08:06.780
The network, network in/network out,

205
00:08:06.780 --> 00:08:09.390
the status checks will be very important to know them.

206
00:08:09.390 --> 00:08:11.190
So you have instance status where you can check

207
00:08:11.190 --> 00:08:13.530
the EC2 VM and system status where you check

208
00:08:13.530 --> 00:08:14.520
the underlying hardware.

209
00:08:14.520 --> 00:08:17.310
And this is AWS that provides these metrics for us.

210
00:08:17.310 --> 00:08:19.980
The disk where we can look at read/write for Ops and bytes

211
00:08:19.980 --> 00:08:21.420
and this is only when you have an EC2

212
00:08:21.420 --> 00:08:22.440
with an instance store,

213
00:08:22.440 --> 00:08:24.390
otherwise you can get these metrics

214
00:08:24.390 --> 00:08:26.250
for the EBS volumes directly.

215
00:08:26.250 --> 00:08:27.510
And something extremely important

216
00:08:27.510 --> 00:08:30.030
that I think I've said all the time in all my courses,

217
00:08:30.030 --> 00:08:32.910
which is that RAM is not included in the EC2 metrics

218
00:08:32.910 --> 00:08:34.710
and you need to push them directly

219
00:08:34.710 --> 00:08:36.750
from the EC2 instance into CloudWatch

220
00:08:36.750 --> 00:08:39.060
as a custom metric if you need it.

221
00:08:39.060 --> 00:08:42.510
Okay, now let's talk about EC2 instance recovery in detail

222
00:08:42.510 --> 00:08:44.280
because it is something you need to know.

223
00:08:44.280 --> 00:08:46.470
The status checks we have, we have two as I said.

224
00:08:46.470 --> 00:08:48.000
The first one is the instance status

225
00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:49.800
and then we have the system status.

226
00:08:49.800 --> 00:08:53.100
And so something we may want to do is to recover an instance

227
00:08:53.100 --> 00:08:55.830
in case these status checks are failing.

228
00:08:55.830 --> 00:08:57.120
So here's what it looks like.

229
00:08:57.120 --> 00:08:59.640
Our EC2 instance is going to be monitored

230
00:08:59.640 --> 00:09:00.810
by a CloudWatch alarm.

231
00:09:00.810 --> 00:09:03.900
And this CloudWatch alarm currently is set up

232
00:09:03.900 --> 00:09:07.350
to be checking the status of the system status check.

233
00:09:07.350 --> 00:09:09.840
And so in case it fails,

234
00:09:09.840 --> 00:09:13.140
you can use your CloudWatch alarm to define an action

235
00:09:13.140 --> 00:09:15.300
called the EC2 instance recovery

236
00:09:15.300 --> 00:09:17.700
to recover your EC2 instance.

237
00:09:17.700 --> 00:09:20.190
The action of using an EC2 instance recovery

238
00:09:20.190 --> 00:09:22.920
is that you will remain, obviously some things will be lost

239
00:09:22.920 --> 00:09:26.820
but you will keep the same private IP, the same public IP,

240
00:09:26.820 --> 00:09:29.100
the same elastic IP, the same metadata,

241
00:09:29.100 --> 00:09:30.660
and the same placement group.

242
00:09:30.660 --> 00:09:31.770
So this is something quite nice

243
00:09:31.770 --> 00:09:33.720
if you have an instance and it's critical

244
00:09:33.720 --> 00:09:35.190
and you want to make sure you can recover it

245
00:09:35.190 --> 00:09:37.590
in case anything goes wrong at the instance level

246
00:09:37.590 --> 00:09:38.790
or the system level,

247
00:09:38.790 --> 00:09:40.740
in which case setting up a CloudWatch alarm

248
00:09:40.740 --> 00:09:43.620
that will perform an EC2 instance recovery action

249
00:09:43.620 --> 00:09:46.020
is a great way to do it.

250
00:09:46.020 --> 00:09:48.030
Finally, because it's a CloudWatch alarm

251
00:09:48.030 --> 00:09:50.790
it can alert an SNS topic and you can chain it up

252
00:09:50.790 --> 00:09:51.810
with other kind of actions,

253
00:09:51.810 --> 00:09:53.910
for example, for notifying a Slack channel

254
00:09:53.910 --> 00:09:55.080
in case you need to be aware

255
00:09:55.080 --> 00:09:56.980
that your instance has been recovered.

256
00:09:57.960 --> 00:10:00.870
Okay, so that's all you need to know for EC2,

257
00:10:00.870 --> 00:10:01.830
very, very important.

258
00:10:01.830 --> 00:10:04.170
Hopefully this is not too much new stuff for you,

259
00:10:04.170 --> 00:10:06.690
but remember, it's all really important

260
00:10:06.690 --> 00:10:09.090
going into the exam that you know all the details.

261
00:10:09.090 --> 00:10:11.933
All right, that's it, I will see you in the next lecture.

